{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-125",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Positive isolated conducting sphere: twelve exterior radial field rays, twelve surface charge marks, and a non-field radius indicator. Total charge is not an interior point charge.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "/Users/yong/.codex/worktrees/stemrobin-181-six/lemmadeck/ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/9p/figures/fig-125.png",
      "sha256": "94fff93ff85ec4698c77801f1737e6538444fa8c51ab70c4be11c2008b9df1ea"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "自然界和工程中有许多球形或近似球形的物体，例如恒星、行星和雨滴，也有许多近似平面的表面。曲面上一小块区域，在所考察的尺度足够小时，也可以近似看成平面。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "**球面的电场** 考虑半径为$R_0$的孤立金属球。没有外加电场、周围介质均匀时，静电平衡下球面上的电荷均匀分布。球外的电场线沿半径方向分布，图125用带正电的球说明这一结构。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "这里r是观察点到球心的距离。球外的电场线形状与位于球心、带相同总电荷的点电荷相同，见图126。场线形状相同本身并不能确定场强大小；还必须使用相同总电荷和球对称条件。因此，在均匀的周围介质中，球外场强可由点电荷公式(8-13)确定。原书写作$r\\geq R_0$；在球面处，以下公式表示从外侧趋近表面时的场强。在国际单位制中，"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "图125"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "图126"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{|q|}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon r^2}$。 (8-16)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "静电平衡时，金属球内部，即$r<R_0$处，场强等于零。上式中的介电常数因子依次是真空介电常数和周围介质的相对介电常数。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "**平面的电场** 均匀带电表面的等面积区域带有相同电荷。原书以每平方厘米所带的电荷说明这种均匀性；单位面积上的电荷称为电荷面密度$\\sigma$。若电荷$q$均匀分布在面积$S$上，则"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "$\\sigma=\\frac{q}{S}$。 (8-17)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "考虑两侧处于同一种均匀介质中的理想无限大均匀带电薄平面。其电场线彼此平行，并垂直于平面，见图127。每一侧的电场都是匀强电场，场强大小与到平面的距离无关；两侧场强大小相等、方向相反。正电荷的场线离开平面，负电荷的场线指向平面。场强大小由电荷面密度$\\sigma$确定。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "球面或点电荷场强随$q/r^2$所表示的电荷与距离关系变化；无限大平面的场强大小则与电荷面密度的绝对值成正比。按上述理想模型计算，可得到以下结果。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "图127"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "在国际单位制中，电荷面密度用库仑每平方米表示，"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{|\\sigma|}{2\\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon}$。 (8-18)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "在原书使用的静电单位制中，必须使用该单位制的电荷面密度和场强单位，不能把国际单位制数值直接代入，"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{2\\pi|\\sigma|}{\\varepsilon}$。 (8-19)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:zh",
        "text": "现实中不存在无限大的平面。对于有限的均匀带电平面，如果观察点远离边缘，而且到平面的距离远小于平面的横向尺寸，就可以近似使用无限大平面的结果。近边缘处不能使用这一近似。各小块电荷的场强随距离平方的倒数衰减，远处边缘区域对所考察点的影响较小。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Nature and engineering contain many spherical or nearly spherical objects, such as stars, planets, and raindrops, as well as approximately flat surfaces. A small region of a curved surface can also be approximated as a plane when the scale being considered is sufficiently small."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "**The field of a spherical surface** Consider an isolated metal sphere of radius $R_0$. With no applied external field and a homogeneous surrounding medium, its charge is uniformly distributed over the surface at electrostatic equilibrium. The external field lines follow radial directions; Figure 125 illustrates this structure for a positively charged sphere."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Here r is the distance from the center of the sphere to the observation point. Outside the sphere, the field-line pattern is the same as that of a point charge at the center with the same total charge, as shown in Figure 126. A matching line pattern alone does not determine field magnitude: the same total charge and spherical symmetry are also needed. In a homogeneous surrounding medium, the external field is therefore given by the point-charge formula (8-13). The source writes $r\\geq R_0$; at the surface, the following expression means the limiting field approached from outside. In SI units,"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Figure 125"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Figure 126"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{|q|}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon r^2}$. (8-16)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "At electrostatic equilibrium, the field inside the metal sphere, where $r<R_0$, is zero. The permittivity factors in the preceding formula are the vacuum permittivity and the relative permittivity of the surrounding medium, respectively."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "**The field of a plane** Equal areas of a uniformly charged surface carry equal charges. The source describes this uniformity using the charge per square centimeter; charge per unit area is called the surface charge density $\\sigma$. If charge $q$ is uniformly distributed over area $S$, then"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "$\\sigma=\\frac{q}{S}$. (8-17)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Consider an ideal infinite, uniformly charged thin plane with the same homogeneous medium on both sides. Its field lines are parallel and perpendicular to the plane, as shown in Figure 127. The field is uniform within each half-space, and its magnitude is independent of distance from the plane. The magnitudes on the two sides are equal, but the directions are opposite. Lines point away from positive charge and toward negative charge. The magnitude is determined by the surface charge density $\\sigma$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "For a sphere or point charge, the charge-and-distance dependence is represented by $q/r^2$; for an infinite plane, field magnitude is proportional to the absolute value of the surface charge density. Calculation for the ideal model described above gives the following results."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "Figure 127"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "In SI units, with surface charge density measured in coulombs per square meter,"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{|\\sigma|}{2\\varepsilon_0\\varepsilon}$. (8-18)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "In the electrostatic unit system used by the source, use that system throughout for charge density and field strength; do not substitute SI numerical values directly,"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "$E=\\frac{2\\pi|\\sigma|}{\\varepsilon}$. (8-19)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy9-c2-s2-n70:en",
        "text": "No real plane is infinite. For a finite uniformly charged plane, the infinite-plane result is a useful approximation when the observation point is well away from the edges and its distance from the plane is much smaller than the lateral dimensions of the plane. The approximation does not apply near an edge. Contributions from small charged regions decrease with the inverse square of distance, so distant edge regions have a smaller influence at the point being considered."
      }
    ],
